\section{Mortality}{
 Use \code{calculate_mortality_rates()}: Reports deaths per
person-year lived.
}

\section{Fertility}{
 Use \code{calculate_fertility_rates()}: Reports maternal
births per woman-year, including time excluded by minimum birth spacing.
Only female rows are returned. Current parity is reconstructed from all
recorded maternal births, including pre-window births and deceased
children. Setting \code{unrealized = TRUE} will calculate a separate
\code{rate_fertility_adjusted} column that calculates fertility rates as if
unrealized births were realized.
}

\section{Union formation}{
 Use \code{calculate_union_rates()}: Reports union starts
(both marriage and cohabitation) per unpartnered person-year lived.
Marriages that occur because of the conversion of a cohabitation are not
counted as events in this function. To calculate cohabitation and direct
marriage separately, use \verb{by = "union_type}.

The \code{age_breaks} argument is always required for this function, but
\code{dur_breaks} can also be used when stratified by \code{previous_union} to get
duration-based rates for union formation since the previous marriage for
individuals who have been previously married.
}

\section{Marriage}{
 Use \code{calculate_marriage_rates()}: Reports direct marriages
and conversions to marriage from cohabitation over nonmarried person-years.
Unpartnered and cohabiting time both contribute exposure. Married time
contributes none.
}

\section{Dissolution}{
 Use \code{calculate_dissolution_rates()}: Reports union
dissolutions over union-years lived. To calculate dissolution separately by
marriage and cohabitation, use \code{by = "union_type"}.

Dissolution can only be calculated using \code{dur_breaks} where duration is
measured since the formation of the current union.
}

\section{Cohabitation conversion}{
 Use \code{calculate_cohab_convert_rate()}:
Reports conversions from cohabitation to marriage over cohabitation
union-years.
}

\section{Group transitions}{
 use \code{calculate_transition_rates()}: Reports state
transitions from origin to destination category per person-years lived in
origin category.

When more than one group is in use in a simulation, the \code{group} argument
must be used to specify which group transition rates are desired.
Transition rates can be stratified by other groups besides the focal group.

Transition rates can be defined by \code{age_breaks} or \code{dur_breaks}. If
\code{dur_breaks} is used, The duration for the first transition is equivalent
to age.
}

